cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this repository or its results, please cite the work below."
type: software
title: "Data and code for: What a sample's energy certifies about quantum-generated training labels"
authors:
  - family-names: "Bonilla Vargas"
    given-names: "Nicolás"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0006-6155-4391"
    affiliation: "Universidad Nacional de Colombia, Bogotá, Colombia; SRH University, Munich, Germany; Daita AI, Munich, Germany"
    email: "ngbonillav@unal.edu.co"
repository-code: "https://github.com/nicolasbonilla/energy-certified-quantum-labels"
url: "https://github.com/nicolasbonilla/energy-certified-quantum-labels"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24216375"
version: "1.0.3"
date-released: "2026-11-14"
abstract: >-
  Replication package of the article "What a sample's energy certifies about quantum-generated
  training labels". Abstract of the article: Quantum processors now supply training labels for machine
  learning of quantum matter; each sample's energy is a scalable quality check. We determine, label by
  label, what any energy-only certificate can guarantee and the accuracy it requires. A label stays
  certifiable at large size exactly when shifting it costs energy linear in size: local labels lose
  certification beyond a size inversely proportional to the energy error per site; averages keep it
  under a uniform-gap hypothesis. For the model of a public 57- and 115-qubit dataset we prove the
  hypothesis at strong anisotropy, above a threshold that is not explicit; in the dataset's range it
  remains a hypothesis. The published energy and site magnetizations, taken as those of one state,
  certify in exact arithmetic, with no reference value, the order parameter to 6% at 26 of 45 training
  couplings at 57 qubits and 21 at 115 (27 with certificates added afterwards), where an exact 19-site
  calculation is within 1.3% of the reference. Near the isotropic point, where the test couplings lie,
  the order parameter needs an energy error more than 110 times smaller at 57 qubits (79 at 115);
  explicit states show that no analysed label is certified. The public labels are over-ordered there;
  at 57 qubits a cubic model trained on them inherits the bias: its standard prediction intervals
  contain 41 of 45 held-out labels but the authors' reference value at none (11 of 45 at 115).
  Intervals calibrated on the 18 labels certified to half the tolerance stay below the tolerance and
  contain the reference value, which varies little there, in all 200 splits, also for a control
  trained on uncertified labels; with all certified labels they mostly exceed it. One processor point
  of ours is certified with no reference value, where a classical energy does better. We propose that
  datasets report each label's interval width alongside the best classical estimate.
